<p>Flood damage insurance claims in Dallas usually start with flash flooding, not a slow-rising river. NOAA lists 305 flash-flood reports in Dallas County, compared with 22 general flood entries. Heavy rain on clay soils and paved surfaces can overwhelm storm drains, push creeks out of their banks, and send water into homes, apartments, and businesses within hours.</p>
<p>The most important question on any flood claim is which policy applies. This page explains the difference between flood and other water damage, how flood policies work, and how a public adjuster in Dallas can help you document the loss.</p>

<h2>The August 2022 Dallas Flash Flood</h2>
<p>On August 21&ndash;22, 2022, upwards of 15 inches of rain fell in parts of Dallas (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1053557" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). NOAA reports include more than 10 inches in a few hours in Mesquite, where I-635 at Scyene Road closed, water rescues in Balch Springs, two feet of water inside an east Dallas apartment complex, and more than 42 roads with high water reported by Dallas police. Flooding like this can reach properties well outside mapped high-risk areas.</p>
<p>Flood risk in Dallas County follows its waterways: the Trinity River and its downtown levee system, White Rock Creek and White Rock Lake, Turtle Creek, Five Mile and Ten Mile creeks, Rowlett Creek, and the shores of Lake Ray Hubbard. Low-lying streets and underpasses flood often during intense storms.</p>

