<h2>Documenting a Flood Claim</h2>
<p>Flood claims depend on showing how high the water rose and what it touched. Helpful steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Photograph the water line on walls, doors, and furniture before cleanup.</li>
<li>Take photos and video of every room, including closets, cabinets, and the garage.</li>
<li>Keep samples of flooring, carpet, and drywall when practical.</li>
<li>Make a contents list with descriptions, ages, and replacement prices.</li>
<li>Keep receipts for water extraction, drying, and mold prevention.</li>
<li>Record flood-related street closures and NOAA reports for your area on our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a fuller list.</p>

<h2>Pier-and-Beam and Older Homes</h2>
<p>Many older Dallas neighborhoods, including Lakewood, the M Streets, Oak Cliff, and Lower Greenville, have pier-and-beam homes with crawl spaces. Floodwater in a crawl space can soak insulation, ductwork, and subflooring. Flood policies limit what they cover below the lowest elevated floor, so it is important to understand those limits and document the space thoroughly. Slab-on-grade homes face different issues: water wicking into drywall, baseboards, and cabinetry. On either type, moisture readings taken during drying help show how far the water traveled and which materials need replacement rather than cleaning. Keep copies of the drying logs your mitigation company records.</p>

<h2>Common Flood Claim Disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flood vs. covered water.</strong> The property insurer says all the water was flood, while the owner believes some came through a storm-damaged roof or a plumbing failure. Separate documentation of each source helps.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-existing damage.</strong> Mold, rot, or settling is attributed to long-term moisture rather than the flood event.</li>
<li><strong>Scope of drying and tear-out.</strong> The estimate allows less drywall, insulation, or flooring removal than the water height and drying readings support.</li>
<li><strong>Contents valuation.</strong> Items are paid at depreciated value, or some items are disputed as excluded or limited under the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Missed deadlines.</strong> Proof-of-loss and appeal deadlines on flood policies can be strict, so calendar them early.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a payment seems too low for the actual repairs, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, helps property owners across Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities with flood and water losses. We can help identify which policies may apply, document building and contents damage, prepare a detailed estimate, and organize proof-of-loss documents. We cannot guarantee an outcome, and NFIP claims follow federal rules. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Safety and Next Steps</h2>
<ol>
<li>Do not enter flooded buildings until it is safe; watch for electrical hazards.</li>
<li>Report the loss to your flood insurer and your property insurer if both may apply.</li>
<li>Document before removing damaged materials, then begin drying to limit mold.</li>
<li>Check with the City of Dallas Planning &amp; Development Department or your city&rsquo;s permit office before major repairs.</li>
</ol>
